    "q-108434": {
      "dom_id": "step-q108434",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨1,-1,2⟩+w⟨3,4,-1⟩,\\,Π:r=⟨5,3,-4⟩+s⟨1,0,2⟩+t⟨0,-2,3⟩.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "From the equation of a plane in the parametric form $r=p+tv+sw$, we have that its normal vector is $n=v\\times w.$ In our case, the normal vectors to $Π$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =⟨1,0,2⟩\\times ⟨0,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =4i-3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane $Π$ in dot product form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,-3,-2⟩ & =⟨5,3,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨4,-3,-2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,-3,-2⟩ & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨1,-1,2⟩+w⟨3,4,-1⟩$ into the equation of the plane in dot product form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨4,-3,-2⟩ & =19 \\\\ ⟨1+3w,-1+4w,2-w⟩\\cdot ⟨4,-3,-2⟩ & =19 \\\\ 4(1+3w)-3(-1+4w)-2(2-w) & =19 \\\\ 4+12w+3-12w-4+2w & =19 \\\\ 2w & =16 \\\\ w & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $q$ of the intersection point $Q,$ we substitute $w=8$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q & =⟨1+3(8),-1+4(8),2-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨25,31,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $Q(25,31,-6).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
