    "q-33082": {
      "dom_id": "step-q33082",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨2,3,1⟩+w⟨-3,-2,3⟩,\\,Π:r=⟨-15,1,1⟩+s⟨2,0,2⟩+t⟨1,4,0⟩.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "From the equation of a plane in the parametric form $r=p+tv+sw$, we have that its normal vector is $n=v\\times w.$ In our case, the normal vectors to $Π$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =⟨2,0,2⟩\\times ⟨1,4,0⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-8i+2j+8k \\\\  & =⟨-8,2,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane $Π$ in dot product form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-8,2,8⟩ & =⟨-15,1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-8,2,8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-8,2,8⟩ & =130.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨2,3,1⟩+w⟨-3,-2,3⟩$ into the equation of the plane in dot product form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨-8,2,8⟩ & =130 \\\\ ⟨2-3w,3-2w,1+3w⟩\\cdot ⟨-8,2,8⟩ & =130 \\\\ -8(2-3w)+2(3-2w)+8(1+3w) & =130 \\\\ -16+24w+6-4w+8+24w & =130 \\\\ 44w & =132 \\\\ w & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $q$ of the intersection point $Q,$ we substitute $w=3$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q & =⟨2-3(3),3-2(3),1+3(3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7,-3,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $Q(-7,-3,10).$",
