      "kp_id": "e-4944"
    },
    "q-108889": {
      "dom_id": "step-q108889",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:\\frac{x-3}{5}=\\frac{y-1}{2}=\\frac{z+4}{3},\\,Π:r\\cdot ⟨-3,2,-1⟩=39.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "From the equation of the line $l$, we know that it passes through the point $A(3,1,-4)$ and it is parallel to the vector $v=⟨5,2,3⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨3,1,-4⟩+t⟨5,2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3+5t,1+2t,-4+3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨3+5t,1+2t,-4+3t⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨-3,2,-1⟩ & =39 \\\\ ⟨3+5t,1+2t,-4+3t⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,2,-1⟩ & =39 \\\\ (-3)(3+5t)+2(1+2t)-(-4+3t) & =39 \\\\ -9-15t+2+4t+4-3t & =39 \\\\ -14t & =42 \\\\ t & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P,$ we substitute $t=-3$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨3+5(-3),1+2(-3),-4+3(-3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-12,-5,-13⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get $P(-12,-5,-13).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
